ly  mileage as this will give you an idea of  how often you might need to charge for  me i do about 20 miles a day so that  means if i had a pure electric kia nero  i could probably make it the whole week  without having to plug in secondly it's  worth knowing what a longer distance  trip might look like as this will tell  you if you ever need to think about  charging on route now for me that's a  280 mile round trip to go and see my mum  and dad which means that i could  probably make it there without charging  but would need to plug in in order to  make it back home mileage tracking apps  like trip log and mile iq can give you  an idea of how many miles you're  currently driving and if the car you're  looking at doesn't quite cover you for  the range you want try checking out the  department of energy's ev rankings where  you can see which cars offer the most  range fortunately i can tell you this  the average us commute to work is 27  minutes long which means that even if  you drove the whole thing at 60 miles an  hour most modern evs should be able to  get you there and back on a single  charge speaking of charging best figure  out where you're going to plug your new  ev in the third highest reason why  people do not want to think about buying  an ev was that evs are a hassle and i  think charging times really figure into  that if you're lucky enough to have a  garage or a parking space on your  property then you can plug straight into  a standard outlet but be warned charging  through a household outlet is far slower  than through a dedicated ev charger if  you want faster charging you might want  to invest in something called a level 2  charger which will charge your vehicle  quicker but if you don't have a parking  space you're probably going to have to  find a public charger fortunately  websites like plug share can let you  figure out where your nearest public  charger might be and it can be used to  help you find charging points on longer  journeys too if 